{"name":"Woven Raffia","hex":"#f1dfc0","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"woven","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Fabricated by weaving.","Interlaced"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cloth formed by weaving. It only stretches in the bias directions (between the warp and weft directions), unless the threads are elastic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/woven"},{"term":"raffia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fibrous material used for tying plants, originating from the leaves of raffia palm trees (genus Raphia)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/raffia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Woven fabric","blocks":[{"text":"Woven fabric is any textile formed by weaving. Woven fabrics, often created on a loom, are made of many threads woven in a warp and weft. Technically, a woven fabric is any fabric made by interlacing two or more threads at right angles to one another."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Palaung_Woman_Kalaw_Shan_Myanmar.jpg/330px-Palaung_Woman_Kalaw_Shan_Myanmar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woven_fabric"},{"title":"Raffia palm","blocks":[{"text":"Raffia palms are members of the genus Raphia. The Malagasy name rafia is derived from fia \"to squeeze juice\". The genus contains about twenty species of palms native to tropical regions of Africa, and especially Madagascar, with one species also occurring in Central and South America. R. taedigera is the source of raffia fibers, which are the veins of the leaves, and this species produces a fruit called \"brazilia pods\", \"uxi nuts\" or \"uxi pods\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Raphia_australis.jpg/330px-Raphia_australis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raffia_palm"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thu